WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.280
You asked for it. Exactly, you
liked it, and we're back with

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:10.720
what gringos think of Brazil,
that foreign view that speaks

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.540
really highly of us, or
maybe not so much, okay?

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.120
Let's see what reputation
Brazilians have abroad.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.260
How do they spot a Brazilian?

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.460
Hey, I'm Fernanda, from California,
and this is my host fam...

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:21.880
Laura.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.400
Okay, Laura, how do you know you
have a Brazilian in the house?

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.040
She's always asking
me, how can I help you?

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.000
How can I help you?

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.180
Even when she's off duty from work,
she's always asking, how can I

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:33.680
help you? Brazilians are like that.
We need to...

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:34.880
Nope, that's not me.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.500
Sorry, that's not me.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.240
I'm more the type who says: if you
need anything, don't count on me.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.320
If you don't need anything, call me.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.880
No, I even say: hey, you
there, need any help?

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.259
You don't need help, do you?

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.680
Anything you need, we're around, right?

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.840
Dinner, or... I'm pretty sure my
mom and dad are very proud of me.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.040
And the other thing is she's always
adding ketchup to everything.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.240
Nope, I'm not that person either.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.760
I don't even keep ketchup at home,
that's not what defines a Brazilian.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.480
Some regions of Brazil
put ketchup on everything.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:08.960
If she's from Rio de
Janeiro, I believe it.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.200
Yeah, looks like it, she puts it on pizza.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.080
Sure, all of Brazil puts it
on pizza, maybe not São Paulo.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.480
No, but we do put it in stroganoff.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.640
The mushroom stroganoff I made here,
I made with ketchup and mustard,

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.960
that's what gives it flavor.
Isn't it tomato sauce?

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.480
No, it's ketchup and mustard.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.680
You can add sauce too, but you add
ketchup for that little sweet touch.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.800
In stroganoff it's part
of the recipe, people.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.080
Look, people, I'm not saying a
word. The comments are all yours,

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.960
I'll stay silent.
No, ketchup is part of the recipe.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.400
All the stuff, stroganoff.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:39.880
Oh, okay. Everything, you're
adding ketchup before you eat.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:45.160
As an Italian, when she saw me
putting ketchup on pizza, she got

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.840
so mad. 'Ana, it's a sin.'

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:47.940
Yes, it's terrible.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.960
And, well, going back to the
food, she's always taking pictures

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.040
of all the food in front of her.
I'm an influencer.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.119
Always eating.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.120
That's not a Brazilian thing.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.640
That's an Instagram thing, man.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.000
Always taking pictures
of what she's eating.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.280
And then the other thing is perfume.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.360
You go into Ana's room and she
has maybe 15 perfumes, like, this

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.960
one's perfume, this one's body splash...

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.680
Yeah, we smell nice.
Because the whole house smells of perfume.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.879
I, I like that.
Brazilians smell good.

00:02:15.919 --> 00:02:17.840
By international standards,
we smell really good.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:18.720
We're nice-smelling people.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.840
That's another Brazilian
thing, we shower a lot.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.480
And wear perfume.
Here, smell. Smells nice.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.280
So, do you wear perfume?

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:26.480
I always wear perfume.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.660
I have allergic rhinitis,
that's why I don't wear perfume.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:31.840
Sorry. I wear perfume for
myself, not for anyone else.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:33.880
Yeah, let me be very clear,
she's not from Rio de Janeiro.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.040
So she's wrong to put
ketchup on her pizza.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.520
The hard life of a Brazilian abroad.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:43.120
POV: Maia tells her gringa friend
that her party is Brazil-themed,

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.060
but then the friend had something
to say about pão de queijo.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.600
The nerve!

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:52.379
There's no way it could be
anything other than Brazil.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.660
I mean, it's your big chance to
find out what a real party is.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.939
Yeah, there's gonna be everything
I brought you to taste.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.339
And there's also gonna be pão de
queijo, which you still haven't tried.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:07.960
What?

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:15.240
Sophie, it's bread with cheese,
crispy on the outside, and on the

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.380
inside, filled with melted cheese.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.160
There's nothing weird about that.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.040
You literally eat hot dogs that are
just bread and sausage, and you dip

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.660
fries in milkshakes.

00:03:29.639 --> 00:03:33.000
You're doubting one of the
main stars of my party.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.660
That's basically an insult.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:37.720
You know what?

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.220
This afternoon, I'm gonna ask my
mom to make me pão de queijo, and

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:43.980
I'll drop some off at your place.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:46.459
Then we'll see if you still
have the same opinion.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:48.939
How dare she?

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.360
Living abroad... That girl there, yeah.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.920
No, no, she's working really
hard to sell the Brazilian party.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.600
It's an endless battle
to convince people, man.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:01.640
And the American thinking
pão de queijo is too exotic.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.000
Looks like she's Canadian, honey.
Made in Canada.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.400
So, Cana... Canadian!

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.779
I've never met a Canadian
that rude, who wouldn't

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.000
buy or even try pão de queijo.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:16.160
They sell pão de queijo at Costco
here, people, it's super famous.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.920
How can she say, 'Oh,
I find the food weird'?

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:19.880
Let me tell you something.
She has no right.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.440
Canada's official food is Japanese, okay?

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:26.640
Canada's most famous dish is
soggy fries with meat gravy,

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:30.000
which is poutine.
Fries wilting in meat gravy.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:31.680
You eat hot dogs at Costco.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:33.600
Do you know what goes into that sausage?

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:35.480
Oh no, the sausage is
really good, I like it.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.520
No, but healthy it is not,
Nilce, let me tell you.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.840
No, but it's 100% beef.
No, what do you count as 'meat' on a cow?

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.519
Let's defend pão de queijo
without offending other cultures.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.860
She likes the hot dog.
I really like the hot dog.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:49.540
When I go buy pão de queijo,
I stop and get a hot dog.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:51.119
Nobody's talking about the hot dog.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.600
You take the least healthy thing humanity
ever made. I add ketchup and mustard.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:57.440
You put it in the bun and eat it.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.680
No, but it's not the same thing, honey.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:01.400
It's not like that sausage.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.360
You've never had it, you can't talk.
You can't talk about what you don't know.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:08.840
The harder life of an immigrant who
got used to Brazil and had to go home.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.120
Now that's real suffering.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:13.640
The shock of the Italian who became
Brazilian and showered in Italy.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:14.680
She learned to shower, huh?

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:20.080
No, just kidding. I'm an Italian
who's lived in Brazil for a year, and

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:21.779
I had forgotten.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:27.520
Suddenly, I took a shower and my skin
turned to paper, my hair to straw,

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:32.960
my body screaming for moisturizer, an
oil, a miracle, and then I remembered:

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.080
the water, the water is hard.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.219
Hard water. Brazil, my love, spoiled me.

00:05:38.279 --> 00:05:40.400
Soft water, shiny hair,

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.379
skin like a peach.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:47.400
Here in Italy, one shower and I
come out exfoliated, dehydrated,

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.840
emotionally shaken. Now
I understand why here,

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:53.799
and 'drama' doesn't even cover it,

00:05:53.839 --> 00:05:59.160
it's one shower a day, if that.
With every drop, my Italian soul

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:04.680
cries, but my Brazilian
skin thanks me for learning

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:05.860
to moisturize.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:11.560
Brazil, you changed me, and
now I need a shower filter

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.239
in my suitcase.

00:06:13.279 --> 00:06:14.880
It's the hard water.

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:16.760
You turn into a lump of plaster.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:18.960
I didn't notice any difference.
But it's not like that here, right?

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:20.920
No, no, in Germany it was.
Oh, it was in Germany too?

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.540
In Germany it was, yeah.
But, honey, you were 20.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:26.499
At 20 you don't notice a thing.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:28.640
The crying starts after 30.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:30.760
I know it builds up in the pipes.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.120
The Irish woman and the Brazilian lunch.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.040
Gringa surprised by the Brazilian lunch.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.440
I embraced Brazilian culture.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:42.379
Of course, it helped that my husband is
Brazilian, but I know lots of foreigners

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:47.759
married to Brazilians, maybe
living in Brazil, but they

00:06:48.279 --> 00:06:52.640
aren't that open to everything
Brazil has to offer, you know?

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.280
In terms of culture.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:58.880
But I embraced the food. I
remember the first day I went down

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:04.480
to the cafeteria and saw it: wow,
there's salad, there's meat, rice,

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:09.600
potatoes. I'm not hungry for
this at 11, because my belly,

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:13.100
my Irish belly, my stomach wasn't used to

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.520
this kind of food.
Like a big lunch.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.120
How do you say it...
real food, proper food.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:19.340
Yes. Real food.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:22.460
Okay, if they're eating it, I'll try it.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:26.920
I remember, I took rice, some
beans, some meat, a salad,

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:27.960
and went off to eat, right?

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:31.259
Then I was teaching English
and almost fell asleep.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:34.360
Heavy belly. Heavy belly.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:38.840
I thought, no, I can't
eat like this every day.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.680
After 1 or 2 years in Brazil,

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.320
my body was already craving
a, a kind of food...

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.800
Something heartier, right? Yes.
as they say in Brazil.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:51.320
Yeah, hearty. But I'll tell you one
thing, this is one of the problems

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:52.000
with Brazil.

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:57.800
Brazilian lunch, with that
heat after midday, that, that,

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:01.800
that mountain we pile up at the
buffet, you get nothing done, okay?

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:04.040
You'll only start doing
things right around 4 in the

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:07.499
afternoon. But I think it's
nice to have a siesta time.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:10.760
The problem isn't lunch, the
problem is the work routine.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:14.940
But, like, Brazilian food, man, when
you move abroad, it's the biggest shock,

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:16.160
the biggest pain, really.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.960
At least in the other
countries I've been to so far,

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.640
you eat a real meal, like our
Brazilian lunch, at dinner, usually.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:23.680
And that's if you're lucky.

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:25.800
Yeah, you don't eat...
even lunch is small.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.400
Yeah, lunch is usually a sandwich
you eat walking down the street.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:31.960
It's in Brazil that we have this
habit of having a giant lunch,

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.200
with lunch being the main meal.

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:36.160
And that's right.
Breakfast and lunch.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:39.080
But lunch, it marks the day.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.600
A day without lunch is sad, right?

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.360
We praise Brazilian lunch, but I'm going
to do some Brazilian self-bashing here.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.300
Our breakfast, compared to
other places in the world, isn't

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:52.080
all that great. No, speak
for yourself, young padawan.

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:53.000
No, what do we eat?

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:57.240
A bread roll with butter and
coffee is very good for the soul.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:08:59.160
Nobody's saying it's good.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.480
The soul's in there too,
look, it needs feeding.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:05.240
Some countries eat vegetables in the
morning, cucumber, tomato, like the folks

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:09.760
in the Middle East.
You've got fish with the East Asian crowd.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:12.800
Brazilian breakfast, I know
it's good, because we eat it

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:13.880
every day. We like it.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.360
No, you just make an egg, then
put it in the middle of the bread.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:18.160
Done, now it's healthy.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:19.880
That's not how it works. Yes, it is.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.000
Our breakfast isn't one of the best.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:22.639
That's the truth.

00:09:22.679 --> 00:09:24.680
No, no. Let's quit the
Brazilian self-bashing.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:26.960
It's self-bashing, and we have
the right to bash ourselves too.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.960
The main culture shock: New York
hot dog versus the hot dog from

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:34.160
Osasco. Welcome to the Big Apple.
What can I get started for you?

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:36.320
Uh, can I get a hot dog, please?

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:39.240
Sure thing, coming right up.
Alright, here you go.

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:41.440
Here's your typical
New York-style hot dog.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:44.040
Where's the fun in that, people?
And your ketchup.

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:45.720
Just a boiled sausage.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:49.079
Yeah, if you want a more loaded
hot dog, you need to go to Brazil.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:52.540
Then he caught a plane.
Went to Brazil.

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:54.760
Flew Azul, huh?
Welcome to Brazil.

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:57.880
Thanks. Can I have a hot dog, please?

00:09:57.920 --> 00:09:59.540
We're gonna start off with our bun.

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:02.600
We're gonna put some mayonnaise.
Exactly.

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:05.080
One hot dog in the sauce that I made.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:06.700
The sauce consists of

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:11.960
onions, tomato sauce, garlic
paste, salt and pepper.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:15.560
I'm gonna add a little bit
more of this delicious onion

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:17.240
tomato sauce up top.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:21.659
It smells so good. That garlic, potent.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:23.940
We're going to add some corn,

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:26.979
peas, and the star of the show,

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:29.040
homemade mashed potatoes.

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:35.080
Now the mashed potatoes act as a binder
for all these delicious ingredients.

00:10:35.559 --> 00:10:36.200
That's right.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:37.639
Where are the potato
sticks and the quail egg?

00:10:37.679 --> 00:10:38.120
It's going on top.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:39.960
Where are the potato
sticks and the quail egg?

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.899
No, you have to add the potato sticks.
And a little bit of crunch.

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:47.519
I cannot wait to put this
Brazilian hot dog in my mouth.

00:10:47.559 --> 00:10:50.320
Enjoy. But it was supposed to be for me.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:51.780
I ordered the hot dog.

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:54.019
Delicious. I'll go make you another one.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:56.800
Thank you to all the Brazilian
followers who reached out to us to make

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:58.600
this hot dog.
It is so delicious.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:00.059
You guys were so right.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:02.480
All the ingredients were top-notch.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:04.819
Making the hot dog here at home

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:08.400
was awesome. Unfortunately, here in
Chicago, I could not find a place to go

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:11.260
get a Brazilian hot dog.
No problem, I made it at home.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:14.960
That's it, because it's homemade,
you make it at home. That's it.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:16.379
A round of applause.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:20.380
My mouth is watering over here.

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:22.120
Respect your gringa.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:23.960
The accent I picked up
when I learned Portuguese.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:28.440
I find it so unfair that learning
Portuguese meant I could have any

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:31.240
accent, and I ended up
with a countryside accent.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:32.300
Imagine.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:36.680
Brother, Rio de Janeiro...
forget about it.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:38.160
Oh, that would be so cool.

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:41.160
I don't get why she didn't
like the countryside accent.

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:42.940
What about me, from Birigui?

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:45.320
She got it 50% right, okay?

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:48.600
Yeah, 50%. Because the Rio de
Janeiro accent isn't better either.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:51.040
The accent from inland
Rio... Oh, knock it off!

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:54.400
The one you're hearing right
here is the Rede Globo accent.

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:57.800
Look, you hear this
accent on Jornal Nacional.

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:00.480
They'll agree. You have no shame, do you?

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:03.059
But it's true.
No shame at all.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:06.480
The Canadian has a point.

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:09.519
My God, foreigners eating.
He learned rice, he learned

00:12:09.559 --> 00:12:11.500
pasta. What's that?

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:15.080
Potato. Potato, meat.
Meat. There's sausage there.

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:17.459
I hate my country.

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:21.179
I don't know if you watch
my videos, but in Canada

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:23.979
it's very cold.
Yeah. Minus 30.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:26.920
Yeah, he must be from Toronto.
Or Alberta.

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:29.760
Or Montreal.
Today we're having barbecue.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:34.620
Like, imagine me stuck at home
for 8 months because of snow.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:37.920
Yeah, he must live in
the middle of Canada.

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:44.740
Like, imagine no dogs in the
street, uh, no loud music at night.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.380
Yeah, there really isn't.
Oh, but that's good.

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:50.320
Even the people are really cold, right?

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:52.200
Like, there's no way to
make friends on the street.

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:53.740
Oh yes there is, we did.

00:12:55.559 --> 00:12:56.639
A Brazilian. Yeah.

00:12:56.679 --> 00:13:00.160
In Canada, we have none
of this. The food...

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:02.200
...is really bad. Sandwiches for lunch.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:06.320
It really is bad.
I have to say he's absolutely right.

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:08.880
Yeah. I'm looking at his plate, man.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.900
The food in my videos isn't
that good, I'm not happy.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:14.160
Here, I'm very happy.

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:17.160
You all say I'm crazy for
leaving my country to come to

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:19.859
Brazil. I really am crazy, I love Brazil.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:25.720
Mmm. Thank you, Brazil,
for another tasty meal.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:28.179
What's that?
Is that potato?

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:30.859
The guy destroyed that plate.

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:34.519
Thank you, Brazil. Thank you, Brazil.

00:13:34.559 --> 00:13:37.440
Brazilian food is really good.
There's no way around it, people, no way.

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:42.280
Look at that: beans, rice, pasta,
the amount of meat you piled on that

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:44.120
plate. But just a tip, buddy, okay?

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:46.760
The beans go on top of
the rice. Since you're

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:48.000
a foreigner, I'll let it slide.

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:50.200
But I get what he did
there, so the beans don't

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:52.560
touch the potato.
Or the pasta.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:56.160
Yeah, he used the rice as a barrier.
Congrats, Michael Maino.

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:57.780
Now I'm jealous of you, you know?

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:01.320
Canada very cold.
Very cold, Canada.

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.080
Very cold, Canada.
That really is different.

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:06.560
My Russian mom was
impressed by this in Brazil.

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:09.420
The Russian was impressed
by this in Brazil.

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:14.260
Last week I got my driver's license
and, of course, sent a photo to

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:17.880
my parents so they
could celebrate with me.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:23.120
Then my mom remarked on how
important family is to Brazilians.

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:26.440
Even the driver's license
shows the parents' names.

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:32.000
In Russia, parents are only listed
on the birth certificate, which

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:34.420
we only use until age 14.

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:38.840
After that, documents don't
carry that information.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:39.720
Same here, right?

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:43.840
Here, yeah, but Brazil must have some
reason for it, because... Could it be

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:47.120
lots of people with similar names?
Probably, for starters.

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:49.000
It must be because of
people with the same name.

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:53.680
Yeah, it must go back to a time when
you'd use the father to identify

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:56.920
people. I don't know when surnames
really caught on in Brazil.

00:14:56.960 --> 00:15:00.020
Patronymic is when you refer to
the parents to know who a person

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:01.840
is. So-and-so, son of so-and-so.

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:06.240
But really, that family bond
in Brazil is more visible

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:09.360
in the culture,
in how long kids live at home,

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:12.320
and so on. Exactly.
We're glued together, from early on.

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:15.620
Any culture shock?
Did we get any culture shock from Brazil?

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:20.520
The biggest shock, a real shock, was
seeing kids take part in their parents'

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:25.480
social life late into the night,
because in England kids go to bed really

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:30.299
early. And I saw kids, like,
at family parties, you know?

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:35.000
9, 10, 11 at night, kids still
playing in the street while their

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:39.680
parents socialize, which I think is
cool because they're part of their

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:41.979
parents' lives, but it was a real shock.

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:45.740
And one shock was coming
across the pressure cooker.

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:48.200
We don't really have
pressure cookers in Peru.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:49.599
It's not very common.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:52.280
But other than that, we
have a lot in common.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:55.040
That whole Latin warmth thing.

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:57.900
But the question is, do you know
how to use a pressure cooker yet?

00:15:58.280 --> 00:15:59.920
No, I don't know how.
I do.

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:01.880
So she's more Brazilian than I am.

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:05.360
Yeah, this kid thing really is...

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:08.440
here it's kind of like Brazil, right?
We see kids with their parents.

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:09.440
No, not at night.

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:12.920
Here kids go to bed really early, they
don't join their parents' plans at

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:13.560
night. They don't?

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:17.040
In Brazil it's super common,
you go to a restaurant at night,

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:17.840
you take the kids along.

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:20.840
True, we don't see kids at
night when we go out to dinner,

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:22.960
right? Here, no kids.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:24.640
Usually only foreigners.

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:26.720
In Brazil, you drag the kid everywhere.

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:30.400
My dad would watch the Japanese Formula
1 Grand Prix in the middle of the night,

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:32.680
he needed someone to
talk about the race with.

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:34.560
Look, you're staying up till 2 a.m., kid.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:35.600
How cool.

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:39.320
Now, pressure cookers really aren't
a thing here, we have to cook

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:42.360
in an electric cooker
and it's not the same.

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:46.100
I made braised beef the other
day, I used two pots, one to sear

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:50.760
the meat, because here that doesn't
work, and then I had to leave it in the

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:51.920
electric cooker, which is constant.

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:53.880
There's no such thing
as a pressure cooker.

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:55.520
The electric one is a pressure cooker.

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:57.440
But the heat is different.
It works exactly the same.

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:02.360
It's not the same, with the electric
one you can't adjust the strength of the

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:04.280
flame, and that makes a
difference in the recipe.

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:06.480
Funerals really are quite different.

00:17:06.520 --> 00:17:10.500
One of the things that
surprised me most in Brazil,

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:13.540
this will sound weird,
but it's the funerals.

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:15.920
Let me explain.
They took the gringa to a wake.

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:21.120
In Brazil, someone had
died, and the next day

00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:22.980
my ex-boyfriend's family

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:26.300
was going to the wake.

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:27.480
I was like,

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:31.940
the next day?
What do you mean, the next day?

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:36.680
That's when I realized how
different it is from here in Canada.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:37.720
Guys!

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:42.180
As I understood it, in Brazil, when
someone passes, by the next day there's

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:45.539
a wake, and, like, it's
way too fast and everyone

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:48.720
has to come.
And if you're not there, you're not there.

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:51.739
In Canada, when someone dies, it takes

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:54.760
weeks and even months.

00:17:54.800 --> 00:17:55.959
Whoa, months?

00:17:56.359 --> 00:18:00.840
Like, months? Just to give you an
idea, when my grandma passed, it was

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:04.360
during Covid, okay?
Oh, but... It took almost a year.

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:08.400
Even worse, my grandpa who passed
lived in the United States and we

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:10.779
live in Canada.
When he died,

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:14.960
they sent his ashes by mail.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:16.799
Very different from Brazil, isn't it?

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:18.899
Say what? Are you crazy?

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:22.960
What? The old man died and
they just mailed the ashes.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:24.000
Shocked.

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:26.560
We've never been to a funeral
here, I don't know what it's like.

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:28.360
I really don't know either.

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:31.180
In movies we...
I've heard stories, but I've never been.

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:33.079
Nobody I know has died here.

00:18:33.119 --> 00:18:36.460
Yeah, but from movies
we know the culture...

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:39.600
Queen Elizabeth died and it took a
while for her funeral. Oh, but she's the

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:40.480
queen. But that's what I'm saying.

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:42.920
No, but we know from movies
that it takes a while.

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:45.480
Here... no, they prepare a ceremony,

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:49.400
everyone goes, people get dressed
up, the whole thing. Must take a few

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:50.480
days, at least.

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:56.120
But I didn't know it was weeks, months.
That's a bigger scale, didn't expect it,

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:59.400
right? Brazil, it's the next day, the
same day. Brazilians don't waste time.

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:02.040
That's not the point, right?
It's the conditions for keeping the body.

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:04.920
First, the conditions for keeping it,
and it must be historical, because of the

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:06.079
climate. Exactly. Right?

00:19:06.119 --> 00:19:09.960
Because before refrigeration was
invented, you had to hurry things along.

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:13.720
In much of the US and North
America, you could probably wait

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:16.000
a bit longer.
In winter, you could wait a long time.

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:19.720
Yeah, the Brazilian over there saying:
here in Brazil, after 3 months, the

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:21.419
widower is already remarried.

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:25.579
What are you laughing at?

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:30.920
Next. They still haven't
discovered this magic.

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:32.319
I don't get these people either.

00:19:32.359 --> 00:19:35.560
My American boyfriend outraged at
milk that isn't kept in the fridge.

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:39.459
What, milk not in the
refriger... refrigerator?

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:41.059
What's that, 'refrigerator'?

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:44.259
Geladeira. Geladeira. Why not?

00:19:44.560 --> 00:19:46.760
Because that's how it
is here in Brazil, love.

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:49.880
There's a different
pasteurization process.

00:19:49.920 --> 00:19:52.468
Gringo science. I don't
know about America, but

00:19:52.508 --> 00:19:54.239
my father...

00:19:57.320 --> 00:20:00.419
it must be refrigerated, or...

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:02.779
we think it spoils.

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:05.579
Because it really does spoil here, right?

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:07.639
I don't know why, why.

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:11.240
Trying it is good, good, good for me.

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:13.520
Okay, so... It's a
different process, right?

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:15.480
Tetra Pak is a different process.

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:18.880
The carton here is just paper,
it doesn't have that lined layer.

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:22.360
Here's the thing, stuff spoils
because bacteria start digesting it

00:20:22.400 --> 00:20:26.440
from the inside. If you kill
all the bacteria and seal it up,

00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:29.800
so no more bacteria get in... You can't
kill them all, it'll spoil eventually,

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:32.480
but it'll take much longer.

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:34.960
I think that's the science,
or else it was aliens.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:36.920
No, but it really is our thing, right?

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:39.880
Tetra Pak milk.
There's no milk here

00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:41.480
that stays out of the fridge?

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:43.319
Oh no, only the milk
that isn't milk, right?

00:20:43.359 --> 00:20:45.120
Almond milk and stuff like that.

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:47.360
Yeah. In Toronto they still
drink milk from bags, right?

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:49.360
It's kind of cultural,
their trademark here.

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:53.880
Little gringa on her mommy: my 2-year-old
English girl from Brazil's Northeast.

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:55.419
Mommy doesn't say it.

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:58.480
Doesn't she? Hurry, hurry!

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:00.180
Hurry, hurry!

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:02.720
Mommy doesn't say it.

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:05.380
Daddy. He says it, huh?

00:21:05.480 --> 00:21:08.940
Mommy, mommy doesn't say...

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:13.260
He says 'oxente'.

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:17.080
Mommy says it.
She does?

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:24.839
Mommy doesn't say it.

00:21:37.119 --> 00:21:41.899
Aww, how cute!

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:44.939
Hurry, hurry! Oxente!

00:21:45.320 --> 00:21:48.360
Oh my, she's so cute, man.

00:21:48.400 --> 00:21:50.400
Bilingual parenting is a challenge.

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:52.640
The little German girl
caught me with this question.

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:56.480
You said it's 'onion cold',
but onions don't get cold.

00:21:56.520 --> 00:22:00.360
How do I translate Northeastern
Brazilian for my German kids?

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:04.800
No, translate it for me, 'onion
cold', translate it, I'm from the

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:09.720
Southeast, translate it, what
do you mean by 'onion cold'?

00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:11.360
Maybe because it has layers?

00:22:11.400 --> 00:22:13.800
Onion cold, so you end up like an onion?

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:17.960
Could be, no?
Why do you say you're 'drop hungry'?

00:22:18.040 --> 00:22:20.560
A drop? Does a drop get hungry?

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:22.880
'Drop hungry', I'd like to
understand that one too.

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:26.160
I think you're making these expressions
up and saying they're Brazilian

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:29.240
to confuse people. You're just
stringing random words together.

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:31.079
Isn't it 'gota serena'?
Could that be it?

00:22:31.119 --> 00:22:34.520
That thing about...
Why do you call me 'sibito baleado'?

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:36.200
What is 'sibito baleado'?

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:39.039
What's a 'sibito baleado'?

00:22:40.960 --> 00:22:43.840
We're gringos on this one too.

00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:46.640
Sibito baleado. Sibito baleado.

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:49.680
Onion cold, what was the other one?
The 'drop' one? Oh boy.

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:54.579
Drop hungry.
Why do you keep saying 'catfish head'?

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:56.680
I don't know what a catfish is.

00:22:56.720 --> 00:22:59.520
That one I know, that one I know.
Yeah, 'catfish head', we use that.

00:22:59.560 --> 00:23:00.600
That one I know.

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:03.240
So how am I supposed to
explain this to them?

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:05.840
Explain to me, what's a 'sibito baleado'?

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:07.920
I don't know.
What's 'onion cold'?

00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:09.739
Why don't onions get cold?

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:12.280
I share that question.

00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:15.200
No, the 'sibito baleado' is
the most... what is a 'sibito

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:17.960
baleado'? Can we look it up,
please? Now I want to know.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:21.820
An expression from Ceará
for very skinny people.

00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:24.340
That's how you explain what it is.

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:26.560
You just send them to Google.

00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:29.460
Come to think of it, it really
doesn't make much sense.

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:34.200
Guys, after 7 years living in
Brazil, I found out I'd always been

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:37.960
counting on my fingers, you know,
my little hands, the wrong way.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:41.240
This weekend I went to dinner
with my friends, we were

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:46.680
switching topics and one of them started
counting on their fingers, like 1, 2,

00:23:46.720 --> 00:23:49.720
3. And I went, huh?
Something's wrong.

00:23:49.760 --> 00:23:51.760
I asked, why do you count like that?

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:54.099
Everyone looked at me like, because

00:23:54.400 --> 00:23:56.680
this is how we count, right?
We count wrong.

00:23:56.720 --> 00:24:01.960
My whole life, 35 years, I counted
like this, living in Russia.

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:05.240
1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:07.459
Makes sense. My dad counts like that.

00:24:07.520 --> 00:24:10.339
1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:12.680
I can't even move my
fingers to count like that.

00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:15.640
My dad counts like that, maybe
from seeing people abroad?

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:19.460
There's another way.
1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:22.540
No, in Brazil it's 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. I
just don't have the coordination to do

00:24:22.580 --> 00:24:25.079
that. 5? Impossible, guys.

00:24:25.119 --> 00:24:26.800
Brazilians count like Americans.

00:24:26.840 --> 00:24:28.860
I don't know if it's some
influence we have, we don't count

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:31.440
like Europeans.
It's not 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:33.140
In Germany, at least,
they counted like that.

00:24:33.180 --> 00:24:35.560
Brazilians count 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:24:35.600 --> 00:24:38.359
Yeah. Americans count like this.
It really is hard, right?

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:40.720
This way is much easier.
1, 2, 3.

00:24:40.760 --> 00:24:42.639
This finger of mine won't
go up without this one.

00:24:42.800 --> 00:24:45.400
4, 5. But there are different ways.

00:24:45.440 --> 00:24:47.000
I knew about the German one, okay?

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:49.120
I know in the US it's different,
in Brazil it's different, which is

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:50.040
1, 2, 3.

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:52.319
I know there's some place
in Asia, I don't know where,

